WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.510
Film Courage: Something you said in the last question reminds me of the Missed Opportunities

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:08.710
scenario, when I was a little girl there was
a section in our town newspaper where I grew

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:10.150
up about “Missed Opportunities.”

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:15.380
I’ll never forget this one post was about
a man who saw some woman and he described

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:19.320
her in great detail and said “We shared
a moment” and if this is you, can you please

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.430
call me?”

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.880
And I’ve never forgotten that post.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.921
This was pre-Craigslist, and I know all those
posts exist now online.

00:00:26.921 --> 00:00:32.410
But why are we so inthralled with this idea
of missed opportunity?

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:43.850
Pamela Jaye Smith: A missed opportunity is
a factor of idealism because that opportunity

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:44.990
is not fact.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.960
It’s an opportunity.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.260
It’s a possibility.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.360
An onto that we can cast all of our dreams.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.570
And I think most of us have all had those
moments, you glance across a room, you’re

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:02.329
crossing a street you look at them and they
look at you and you go “Yeah….???!!”

00:01:02.329 --> 00:01:03.329
And then you both walk on.

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:07.490
And you think “Should just go stand on that
street corner and wait for them to come by

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:08.549
again?”

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:09.789
And so people do.

00:01:09.789 --> 00:01:19.639
But I think the thing about a missed opportunity
most at its core, it offers that question

00:01:19.639 --> 00:01:23.090
“What If?”

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:29.359
Might that have been perfection that deep
in our hearts we are all striving for?

00:01:29.359 --> 00:01:39.090
Film Courage: So do most romantic comedies
feature the missed opportunity or even if

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:43.180
it’s just a friend at a wedding that they
see across the table that they say hello to.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.029
Isn’t that sort of the core?

00:01:45.029 --> 00:01:46.850
Because of the “What If?”

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:55.099
Pamela Jaye Smith: Yes…the Missed Opportunity
can be the entire storyline of an entire film

00:01:55.099 --> 00:01:57.399
or the main story or the main storyline of
it.

00:01:57.399 --> 00:02:02.919
For instance in FOUR WEDDINGS AND A FUNERAL,
the Hugh Grant character definitely has a

00:02:02.919 --> 00:02:06.159
missed opportunity through line.

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:12.400
And fortunately he resolves it and he and
Andie McDowell get together…again.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:20.879
But within the 3-part structure of the romantic
comedy, that’s the sad part about and the

00:02:20.879 --> 00:02:24.500
tense part about that second section.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.360
Yeah!

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.870
You saw what it could be and now it’s gone.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:36.520
Because one of you had to move or somebody
got in the way or someone else’s impositions

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.530
of propriety are keeping you apart.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:47.349
And so that missed opportunity is a vessel
(if you will) for idealism and perfection

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:59.730
really bothers us and we are driven to pursue
what might be a perfect situation.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:05.650
And now…it’s a romantic comedy and they
always stop when the people get together,

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:06.650
right?

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:11.599
We don’t have to get into the part about
disillusion and domesticity and all that stuff.

00:03:11.599 --> 00:03:17.849
But we get to keep that illusion of perfection
and idealism.

